        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 1473,
            "sid": 3,
            "position_x": 729,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 702,
            "height": 826,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "// divides every value that arrives at its left inlet by the operand and rounds the result DOWN to a whole number. It answers: how many whole times does the operand fit into the input?\n\n7 arriving at \"// 3\" sends out 2 - 3 fits twice, with 1 left over. A sine wave sent through \"// 0.25\" becomes a staircase: it counts quarter-steps, from -4 up to 3.\n\nUse it to count and to quantise: which bar a beat count is in (\"// 4\"), which row of an 8-wide grid a cell number is on (\"// 8\"), which octave a note is in (\"// 12\").\n\nTwo whole numbers give a whole number; a float gives a whole-numbered float (3.0). Arrays and tensors are handled element by element, keeping their shape. A single-number operand applies to every element; an array operand of the same shape pairs element with element.\n\nSYNTAX:\n// <operand: int or float>\n\nEXAMPLES:\n// 4      beat count -> bar number\n// 12     note number -> octave\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe value to divide. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\noperand:\nThe amount to divide by - the size of one step. \nSetting it does not send anything - it waits for the next value at \"in\". \"// 2\" gives a stepped integer slider; \"// 2.0\" a smooth floating point one.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe number of whole steps.\n\nROUNDING DOWN:\n// rounds toward minus infinity, not toward zero, so -7 // 2 is -4, not -3. That keeps every step the same size as the input crosses zero.\n\n// AND % ARE A PAIR:\n// counts the whole steps and % gives what is left over. \"// 8\" on a cell number gives its row in an 8-wide grid; \"% 8\" gives its column.\n\nDIVIDING BY ZERO:\nThis does not raise an error or stop your patch. A single number is divided by 0.00000001 instead, giving a very large number of the right sign. Arrays and tensors give inf (or nan for 0 divided by 0).\n\nSEE ALSO:\n%     the remainder that // leaves behind\n/     divide, keeping the fraction",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 694,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 810,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "// divides every value that arrives at its left inlet by the operand and rounds the result DOWN to a whole number. It answers: how many whole times does the operand fit into the input?\n\n7 arriving at \"// 3\" sends out 2 - 3 fits twice, with 1 left over. A sine wave sent through \"// 0.25\" becomes a staircase: it counts quarter-steps, from -4 up to 3.\n\nUse it to count and to quantise: which bar a beat count is in (\"// 4\"), which row of an 8-wide grid a cell number is on (\"// 8\"), which octave a note is in (\"// 12\").\n\nTwo whole numbers give a whole number; a float gives a whole-numbered float (3.0). Arrays and tensors are handled element by element, keeping their shape. A single-number operand applies to every element; an array operand of the same shape pairs element with element.\n\nSYNTAX:\n// <operand: int or float>\n\nEXAMPLES:\n// 4      beat count -> bar number\n// 12     note number -> octave\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe value to divide. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\noperand:\nThe amount to divide by - the size of one step. \nSetting it does not send anything - it waits for the next value at \"in\". \"// 2\" gives a stepped integer slider; \"// 2.0\" a smooth floating point one.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe number of whole steps.\n\nROUNDING DOWN:\n// rounds toward minus infinity, not toward zero, so -7 // 2 is -4, not -3. That keeps every step the same size as the input crosses zero.\n\n// AND % ARE A PAIR:\n// counts the whole steps and % gives what is left over. \"// 8\" on a cell number gives its row in an 8-wide grid; \"% 8\" gives its column.\n\nDIVIDING BY ZERO:\nThis does not raise an error or stop your patch. A single number is divided by 0.00000001 instead, giving a very large number of the right sign. Arrays and tensors give inf (or nan for 0 divided by 0).\n\nSEE ALSO:\n%     the remainder that // leaves behind\n/     divide, keeping the fraction"
        },
